,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3,3,3],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,3,3,3,3],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,4,4,4,4],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,4,3,3,3],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3,3,3],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The chain pieces swing into the holes (key‑in‑lock) under some rotation or reflection. My inspect version reproduced all three train outputs exactly, and the test results look plausible.\n\n## What the grid is made of\n\nEach input has two kinds of things:\n\n1. **Chains**: two objects of different colours that touch edge to edge.\n   - Pair 1: the 3‑piece sits on top of the 4‑piece.\n   - Pair 3: the 5‑piece touches the 7‑piece.\n2. **Sockets**: a group of same‑coloured objects lying close together that leaves a notch of background between them.\n   - Pairs 1 and 2: the two 2‑bars.\n   - Pair 3: the two 6‑objects.\n   - The **hole** is the set of background cells inside the socket group's bounding box.\n\n## What happens\n\nEach chain is lifted off the grid and placed so that one of its two pieces exactly fills a hole. The other piece sticks out on the open side.\n\nTo make the piece fit, the chain is rotated or reflected as one rigid unit:\n- **Pair 2:** the chain is rotated 90° counter‑clockwise. The 3‑piece fills the 6‑cell hole and the 4‑piece extends to the right.\n- **Pair 3:** the chain is only translated. The 7‑piece fills the 6‑socket's notch.\n- **Pair 1:** the hole is the mirror image of the pair 2 hole, so the 3‑piece fits only after a **reflection**. In the output the two colours are exchanged: the hole is coloured 4 and the protruding 4‑shape is coloured 3.\n\nPair 1 is the only example of a reflected placement, so the colour‑swap rule rests on that single pair. I take it as: a proper rotation keeps the colours, a reflection swaps the chain's two colours.\n\n## Placement search\n\nFor each chain, try all 8 dihedral transforms and both pieces against every hole. Accept a candidate when:\n- the transformed piece covers the hole exactly, and\n- the rest of the chain lands in bounds on cells that are background (or that the chains vacated).\n\nIf several transforms fit, a proper rotation is preferred. This happens in test 2, where the staircase piece is symmetric.\n\n## Applying it to the tests\n\n**Test 1** has two 2‑sockets and two 1/3 chains.\n- The 6‑cell vertical notch at the top left takes the upper chain's 3‑piece, after rotating the chain clockwise.\n- The 8‑cell notch on the right needs the lower chain's 3‑piece reflected vertically. By the swap rule, the hole gets colour 1 and the protruding shape gets colour 3.\n\n**Test 2:**\n- The staircase notch of the 2‑socket takes the 7‑piece; the chain is rotated counter‑clockwise so its 3‑piece stays in bounds.\n- The 5‑cell notch of the 3‑socket takes the 4‑piece, after rotating its chain 180°.\n\n## Teaching stages\n\n1. Erase all chains from the canvas.\n2. Place each chain into its hole, one emit per chain.\n3. Emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    ob = objects(g, bg)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # chains: touching pairs of different-coloured objects\n    ch = [(i, j) for i in range(len(ob)) for j in range(i + 1, len(ob))\n          if ob[i]['colour'] != ob[j]['colour'] and\n          any(abs(a - c) + abs(b - d) == 1 for a, b in ob[i]['cells'] for c, d in ob[j]['cells'])]\n    used = {x for p in ch for x in p}\n    chain_cells = {c for i, j in ch for c in ob[i]['cells'] + ob[j]['cells']}\n    rest = [o for i, o in enumerate(ob) if i not in used]\n    # group socket parts: same colour, within Chebyshev distance 2\n    n = len(rest)\n    par = list(range(n))\n\n    def f(i):\n        while par[i] != i:\n            i = par[i]\n        return i\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if rest[i]['colour'] == rest[j]['colour'] and min(\n                    max(abs(a - c), abs(b - d)) for a, b in rest[i]['cells'] for c, d in rest[j]['cells']) <= 2:\n                par[f(i)] = f(j)\n    gr = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        gr.setdefault(f(i), []).append(rest[i])\n    holes = []\n    for s in gr.values():\n        cells = [c for o in s for c in o['cells']]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        holes.append(frozenset((y, x) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1) if g[y, x] == bg))\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for c in chain_cells:\n        out[c] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Lift every two-colour chain off the canvas; the sockets with their notches remain.\")\n\n    for i, j in ch:\n        cells = ob[i]['cells'] + ob[j]['cells']\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        sub = np.full((y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1), bg)\n        for c in cells:\n            sub[c[0] - y0, c[1] - x0] = g[c]\n        cols = (ob[i]['colour'], ob[j]['colour'])\n        cands = []\n        for fl in (0, 1):\n            for k in range(4):\n                t = np.rot90(np.fliplr(sub) if fl else sub, k)\n                for pc in cols:\n                    pm = [tuple(p) for p in np.argwhere(t == pc)]\n                    for h in holes:\n                        if len(h) != len(pm):\n                            continue\n                        hy = min(y for y, x in h); hx = min(x for y, x in h)\n                        py = min(y for y, x in pm); px = min(x for y, x in pm)\n                        dy, dx = hy - py, hx - px\n                        if {(y + dy, x + dx) for y, x in pm} != set(h):\n                            continue\n                        ok = True\n                        place = []\n                        for y, x in np.argwhere(t != bg):\n                            Y, X = y + dy, x + dx\n                            if not (0 <= Y < H and 0 <= X < W) or (g[Y, X] != bg and (Y, X) not in chain_cells):\n                                ok = False\n                                break\n                            place.append((Y, X, t[y, x]))\n                        if ok:\n                            cands.append((fl, place))\n        if not cands:\n            for c in cells:\n                out[c] = g[c]\n            continue\n        fl, place = cands[0]\n        a, b = cols\n        for Y, X, c in place:\n            out[Y, X] = (b if c == a else a) if fl else c\n        emit(out, \"Rotate/reflect the chain so one piece exactly fills a socket's notch and place it there\"\n                  + (\" (reflected, so the two colours swap).\" if fl else \".\"))\n    emit(out, \"All chains are seated in their sockets.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"I take it as: a proper rotation keeps the colours, a reflection swaps the chain's two colours."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"By the swap rule, the hole gets colour 1 and the protruding shape gets colour 3."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The chain pieces swing into the holes (key‑in‑lock) under some rotation or reflection."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"To make the piece fit, the chain is rotated or reflected as one rigid unit:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 2:** the chain is rotated 90° counter‑clockwise."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 3:** the chain is only translated."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The chain pieces swing into the holes (key‑in‑lock) under some rotation or reflection."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The **hole** is the set of background cells inside the socket group's bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each chain is lifted off the grid and placed so that one of its two pieces exactly fills a hole."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The **hole** is the set of background cells inside the socket group's bounding box."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 1: the 3‑piece sits on top of the 4‑piece."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"168c0a873168fcc3d71f3acc1259404be21aa0516990156fdcaef6b59b1c648e","task_sha256":"bfabccd74167b900c0f83cf26d0db2ff3a812885b3ca2b7e65f93b1b1b728348","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
